Best to "bite the bullet" (if even possible), change the UID
on the second machine to match, and use a find/xargs/chown
pipe as root to find every file owned by your old UID and
make it owned by your new UID instead.  As merely a former
_junior_ Unix sysadmin, I can't envision the situation where
something other than a nethack file transfer will cause you
problems, but with 42 years' computer experience in the
absolute inevitability of Murphy's law proving right, except
for the detail of having sworn off gambling at age twelve,
I'd give some fierce odds that some other file transfer
between the machines will eventually bite you at the world's
most inconvenient possible time because of the mismatch.

xanthian, need I mention this will be dangerous as hell if
fat fingered?  Think backups _first_, think humility next,
and if there's any chance you'll need help, let someone else
do it instead of you.

